   "source": [
    "# House Prices (Ames, Iowa) : my thought proccess is fully explained here. \n",
    "**This version can contain coding mistakes but also reasoning ones. Use your critical thinking and ask yourself questions.** "
    "\n",
    "**Goal**: predict the sale price of a house from its characteristics (surface, quality, neighborhood, year…), and submit the predictions to the Kaggle competition *House Prices: Advanced Regression Techniques*.\n",
    "\n",
    "**How to read this notebook**\n",
    "- Every code cell is preceded by a block **\"What this cell does / Why\"**. Read that first, then the code.\n",
    "- Every important idea (overfitting, Ridge, Lasso, cross-validation…) is explained **the first time it is needed**, in plain English.\n",
    "- There are **no custom Python classes** in this version. Everything uses simple pandas code and a few scikit-learn tools.\n",
    "- A short **glossary** is at the end.\n",
    "\n",
    "**Data**: `train.csv` (1,460 houses **with** their sale price) and `test.csv` (1,459 houses **without** price — Kaggle keeps the answers secret and scores our predictions).\n",
    "\n",
    "**Roadmap**\n",
    "| Part | What we do |\n",
    "|---|---|\n",
    "| 1 | Explore the data (types, missing values, price distribution) |\n",
    "| 2 | Understand what drives the price, remove outliers |\n",
    "| 3 | Prepare the data (missing values, encoding, new features) |\n",
    "| 4 | Train 6 models and compare them on a validation set |\n",
    "| 5 | Compare them more reliably with cross-validation |\n",
    "| 6 | Tune the best model and build an ensemble |\n",
    "| 7 | See which features matter |\n",
    "| 8 | Final model → Kaggle submission file |"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "21445c6c",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## The big picture: what is \"machine learning\" here?\n",
    "\n",
    "This is a **supervised regression** problem:\n",
    "- *Supervised* = we have examples where the right answer is known (houses with their real price) and the computer learns the pattern.\n",
    "- *Regression* = the answer is a **number** (a price), not a category.\n",
    "\n",
    "The learning loop is always the same:\n",
    "1. Show the model many houses with their prices (**training data**).\n",
    "2. The model adjusts itself to make small errors on those houses.\n",
    "3. Check it on houses it has **never seen** (**validation / test data**). That is the only fair way to know if it really learned something useful.\n",
    "\n",
    "The big danger is **overfitting**: a model that *memorises* the training houses (like a student who learns last year's exam answers by heart) but fails on new houses. Almost every technique in this notebook exists to fight overfitting."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "03025a5b",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## Part 0 — Setup"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "97a9ea45",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "**What this cell does**: imports the libraries (toolboxes) we need and fixes a random seed.\n",
    "\n",
    "**Why**\n",
    "- `pandas` = tables (DataFrames). `numpy` = fast maths. `matplotlib` / `seaborn` = charts. `joblib` = save a trained model to disk.\n",
    "- `scikit-learn` (imported as `sklearn`) = all the machine-learning tools. We import them by purpose: splitting data, scaling, models, metrics.\n",
    "- `SEED = 42`: many steps use randomness (shuffling data, building random trees). A fixed seed means **you get the same results every time** you run the notebook.\n",
    "- `warnings.filterwarnings(\"ignore\")` only hides non-critical warning messages to keep the output clean."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 115,
   "id": "a31a2fb5",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "import os, warnings\n",
    "import numpy as np\n",
    "import pandas as pd\n",
    "import matplotlib.pyplot as plt\n",
    "import seaborn as sns\n",
    "import joblib\n",
    "\n",
    "from sklearn.model_selection import train_test_split, KFold, cross_val_score, GridSearchCV\n",
    "from sklearn.pipeline import make_pipeline\n",
    "from sklearn.preprocessing import RobustScaler\n",
    "from sklearn.linear_model import LinearRegression, Ridge, Lasso, RidgeCV, LassoCV, ElasticNetCV\n",
    "from sklearn.ensemble import RandomForestRegressor, GradientBoostingRegressor, VotingRegressor\n",
    "from sklearn.metrics import mean_absolute_error, mean_squared_error, r2_score\n",
    "\n",
    "warnings.filterwarnings(\"ignore\")\n",
    "sns.set_theme(style=\"whitegrid\")\n",
    "random_state = 42 #to reproduce the same result each time we compile"
   ]
  },
