   "source": [
    "### 1.5 The distribution of the sale price (our \"target\")\n",
    "\n",
    "**What this cell does**: draws a histogram of the price, and the same histogram after taking the **logarithm** `log(1 + price)`. It also prints the **skewness** (0 = symmetric; large positive = long tail to the right).\n",
    "\n",
    "**Why a log?**\n",
    "- Prices are **right-skewed**: most houses cost $100k–250k, a few cost $500k+.\n",
    "- A model trained on raw prices is dominated by those few expensive houses (a $50k error on a $500k house counts as much as on a $100k house, although it is proportionally much smaller).\n",
    "- After a log, the distribution is almost symmetric, and errors become **relative** (percentages). That is also exactly how Kaggle scores us (see Part 4, \"RMSLE\").\n",
    "- `log1p(x)` means `log(1 + x)` (safe when x = 0). `expm1` is the exact inverse, to go back to dollars.\n",
    "\n",
    "**From now on the model predicts `log1p(price)`**, and we convert back to dollars at the end."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "7ee1307d",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "Extreme values can have a significant influence on learning, particularly because regression models that use a squared loss function heavily penalize large errors. That's why log(1+x) is used here."
   ]
  },
