   "source": [
    "# Part 3 — Preparing the data\n",
    "\n",
    "The models need a **clean, fully numeric table**. We will do 7 small steps, each in its own cell.\n",
    "\n",
    "### Why there are no classes in this version (and why they existed before)\n",
    "In the previous version, two steps (filling `LotFrontage` by neighborhood, and fixing skewed columns) were written as Python **classes**. That was a technical trick so these steps could be placed *inside* a scikit-learn `Pipeline` and behave like built-in tools:\n",
    "- a \"transformer\" has a `fit` (**learn** something from the training data, e.g. compute medians) and a `transform` (**apply** it);\n",
    "- inside cross-validation, `fit` is then re-run on each training fold only, so nothing from the validation fold \"leaks\" into the learning.\n",
    "\n",
    "It is the \"textbook-perfect\" approach, but it is heavy to read. Here we use a simpler and very common Kaggle practice: **we clean the train and test features together, with plain pandas code**. The statistics we compute (a median, a skewness) use **only the features, never the price**, so no information about the answers leaks. The effect on the score is negligible. (In a real production system you would use the Pipeline/class approach.)"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "ceaf7f6a",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "### 3.1 Stack train and test features into one table\n",
    "\n",
    "**What this cell does**: separates the target (`y = log1p(price)`), then glues the train features and the test features one under the other into a table called `all_data`. We remember `n_train` to cut it back later.\n",
    "\n",
    "**Why**: we apply the exact same cleaning to both, and later we need **identical columns** in train and test (very important after one-hot encoding, because a rare neighborhood might exist only in one of them)."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 124,
   "id": "94c9680f",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [
    {
     "name": "stdout",
     "output_type": "stream",
     "text": [
      "all_data: (2917, 79) (first 1458 rows = train, 1459 last rows = test)\n"
     ]
    }
   ],
   "source": [
    "y = np.log1p(train[\"SalePrice\"])                       # the target, on the log scale\n",
    "test_ids = test[\"Id\"].copy()                           # needed for the submission file\n",
    "\n",
    "features_train = train.drop(columns=[\"Id\", \"SalePrice\"])\n",
    "features_test = test.drop(columns=[\"Id\"])\n",
    "n_train = len(features_train)\n",
    "\n",
    "all_data = pd.concat([features_train, features_test], ignore_index=True)\n",
    "\n",
    "print(\"all_data:\", all_data.shape, f\"(first {n_train} rows = train, {len(features_test)} last rows = test)\")"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "1e2b5fbd",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "### 3.2 Handle missing values\n",
    "\n",
    "**What this cell does**\n",
    "1. Text columns: `NaN` → the label `\"None\"` (= \"feature absent\", see 1.3).\n",
    "2. `MSSubClass` and `MoSold` look like numbers but are really **codes** (class 20, class 60… / month 1–12). Average of \"class 20\" and \"class 60\" has no meaning, so we convert them to text.\n",
    "3. `LotFrontage` (street length in front of the house): fill with the **median of the same neighborhood** — houses on the same street have similar frontage, so this is smarter than one global median.\n",
    "4. `GarageYrBlt` empty (no garage) → use the house's own build year.\n",
    "5. Numeric columns where empty clearly means zero (no basement → 0 sq ft, no garage → 0 cars) → 0.\n",
    "6. Any numeric `NaN` left → the median of the column.\n",
    "\n",
    "**Why median and not mean?** The median is not affected by extreme values (one $700k house would drag the mean up).\n",
    "\n",
    "The last line prints how many `NaN` remain: it must be **0**."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 125,
   "id": "408f4a64",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [
    {
     "name": "stdout",
     "output_type": "stream",
     "text": [
      "Missing values left: 0\n"
     ]
    }
   ],
   "source": [
    "# 1) text columns: NaN -> \"None\"\n",
    "text_cols = all_data.select_dtypes(exclude=\"number\").columns\n",
    "all_data[text_cols] = all_data[text_cols].fillna(\"None\")\n",
    "\n",
    "# 2) numeric codes that are really categories\n",
    "all_data[\"MSSubClass\"] = all_data[\"MSSubClass\"].astype(str)\n",
    "all_data[\"MoSold\"] = all_data[\"MoSold\"].astype(str)\n",
    "\n",
    "# 3) LotFrontage: median of the same neighborhood ('transform' returns one value per row)\n",
    "neighborhood_median = all_data.groupby(\"Neighborhood\")[\"LotFrontage\"].transform(\"median\")\n",
    "all_data[\"LotFrontage\"] = all_data[\"LotFrontage\"].fillna(neighborhood_median)\n",
    "\n",
    "# 4) no garage -> garage year = house year\n",
    "all_data[\"GarageYrBlt\"] = all_data[\"GarageYrBlt\"].fillna(all_data[\"YearBuilt\"])\n",
    "\n",
    "# 5) empty really means \"zero\"\n",
    "zero_cols = [\"BsmtFinSF1\", \"BsmtFinSF2\", \"BsmtUnfSF\", \"TotalBsmtSF\", \"BsmtFullBath\", \"BsmtHalfBath\",\n",
    "             \"GarageCars\", \"GarageArea\", \"MasVnrArea\"]\n",
    "all_data[zero_cols] = all_data[zero_cols].fillna(0)\n",
    "\n",
    "# 6) whatever is left: column median\n",
    "numeric_now = all_data.select_dtypes(include=\"number\").columns\n",
    "all_data[numeric_now] = all_data[numeric_now].fillna(all_data[numeric_now].median())\n",
    "\n",
    "print(\"Missing values left:\", all_data.isna().sum().sum())"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "2add01fa",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "### 3.3 Encode ordered categories as ordered numbers (\"ordinal encoding\")\n",
    "\n",
    "**What this cell does**: many columns are *quality grades* with a natural order: `Po` (poor) < `Fa` (fair) < `TA` (typical) < `Gd` (good) < `Ex` (excellent). We replace each label by a number that respects this order (`None`=0, Po=1, … Ex=5). We do the same for basement exposure, garage finish, etc.\n",
    "\n",
    "**Why**: the number tells the model \"Ex is better than Gd, which is better than TA\". If we had used one-hot encoding (3.6) for these columns, that ordering information would be lost and the model would need more data to rediscover it.\n",
    "\n",
    "`.map(mapping)` replaces labels using the dictionary; `.fillna(0)` gives 0 to any label not in the dictionary."
   ]
