    "### 3.5 Reduce skewness of numeric features (log transform)\n",
    "\n",
    "**What this cell does**: for each numeric column, computes its skewness; if it is strongly skewed (|skew| > 0.75) and has no negative value, applies `log1p`.\n",
    "\n",
    "**Why**: same reason as for the price. Lot sizes, porch surfaces, etc. have a few huge values. A log makes them more regular, which helps **linear models** a lot (they assume roughly straight-line relationships). Tree models do not care. The check `min() >= 0` is needed because `log` is undefined for negative numbers."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 128,
   "id": "1a37f53e",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [
    {
     "name": "stdout",
     "output_type": "stream",
     "text": [
      "40 columns log-transformed, for example: ['LotFrontage', 'LotArea', 'LotShape', 'LandSlope', 'MasVnrArea', 'ExterQual']\n"
     ]
    }
   ],
   "source": [
    "numeric_now = all_data.select_dtypes(include=\"number\").columns\n",
    "skewness = all_data[numeric_now].skew().abs()\n",
    "\n",
    "to_log = [c for c in numeric_now if skewness[c] > 0.75 and all_data[c].min() >= 0]\n",
    "all_data[to_log] = np.log1p(all_data[to_log])\n",
    "\n",
    "print(f\"{len(to_log)} columns log-transformed, for example: {to_log[:6]}\")"
   ]
  },
