   "source": [
    "### 3.6 One-hot encoding of the remaining text columns\n",
    "\n",
    "**What this cell does**: for each text column without natural order (e.g. `Neighborhood` with 25 values), creates **one 0/1 column per value**: `Neighborhood_NridgHt` = 1 if the house is in NridgHt, else 0.\n",
    "\n",
    "**Why not just number the neighborhoods 1, 2, 3…?** Because the model would think neighborhood 25 is \"25 times more\" than neighborhood 1, and that 12 is \"between\" 11 and 13. Neighborhoods have **no order**, so each gets its own yes/no column. This is **one-hot encoding**; `pd.get_dummies` does it in one line.\n",
    "\n",
    "**Rare categories**: a value that appears in fewer than 5 training houses (e.g. one unusual roof material) cannot be learned reliably and only adds noise columns, so we drop those columns."
   ]
