   "source": [
    "# Part 4 — Training and comparing 6 models\n",
    "\n",
    "## 4.1 The evaluation metrics\n",
    "\n",
    "To know whether a model is good, we compare its predictions with the true prices on houses **not used for training**.\n",
    "\n",
    "| Metric | Meaning | Units | Better is |\n",
    "|---|---|---|---|\n",
    "| **MAE** (Mean Absolute Error) | average size of the error, ignoring sign. \"On average we are off by $X.\" | dollars | lower |\n",
    "| **RMSE** (Root Mean Squared Error) | like MAE but squares the errors first, so **big mistakes are punished much more** | dollars | lower |\n",
    "| **R²** | share of the price variation the model explains. 1 = perfect, 0 = no better than always guessing the average | none | higher |\n",
    "| **RMSLE** (Root Mean Squared *Log* Error) | RMSE computed on the **log** of prices = typical error in **percent**. **This is the Kaggle score.** | log units | lower |\n",
    "\n",
    "Because our model already predicts `log1p(price)`, RMSLE is simply the RMSE between predicted and true log-prices. (Rule of thumb: RMSLE 0.11 ≈ typical error of about 11%.)\n",
    "\n",
    "## 4.2 Hold-out validation\n",
    "We keep **20% of the training houses aside** (the *validation set*). Models learn on the other 80% and are judged on this 20%. It is the simplest honest test: exam questions the student has never seen."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "495f0045",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "**What this cell does**: splits the training data 80% / 20% (`train_test_split`; `random_state` makes the split reproducible) and defines a helper `evaluate` that returns the four metrics. It converts predictions back to dollars with `np.expm1` for MAE / RMSE / R², and keeps the log scale for RMSLE."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 132,
   "id": "48983209",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [
    {
     "name": "stdout",
     "output_type": "stream",
     "text": [
      "Learning on 1166 houses, validating on 292 houses\n"
     ]
    }
   ],
   "source": [
    "X_tr, X_val, y_tr, y_val = train_test_split(X, y, test_size=0.2, random_state=random_state)\n",
    "print(f\"Learning on {len(X_tr)} houses, validating on {len(X_val)} houses\")\n",
    "\n",
    "def evaluate(model, X_, y_log_):\n",
    "    pred_log = model.predict(X_)\n",
    "    price_true, price_pred = np.expm1(y_log_), np.expm1(pred_log)     # back to dollars\n",
    "    return {\"MAE ($)\": mean_absolute_error(price_true, price_pred),\n",
    "            \"RMSE ($)\": np.sqrt(mean_squared_error(price_true, price_pred)),\n",
    "            \"R²\": r2_score(price_true, price_pred),\n",
    "            \"RMSLE\": np.sqrt(mean_squared_error(y_log_, pred_log))}"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "7ff3cc41",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## 4.3 Meet the algorithms\n",
    "\n",
    "### Why these models?\n",
    "The project asks for **Linear Regression, Random Forest and Gradient Boosting**. We add **Ridge, Lasso and ElasticNet** because they are the natural upgrade of linear regression for *this* kind of data: few rows (~1,450), many columns (~230 after encoding), many columns that say almost the same thing (e.g. `GarageCars` and `GarageArea`). That is exactly where regularised linear models shine.\n",
    "\n",
    "---\n",
    "### 1) Linear Regression — the baseline\n",
    "Predicts the price as a weighted sum: `log-price ≈ w₀ + w₁·TotalSF + w₂·OverallQual + …`. Training finds the weights that minimise the squared errors.\n",
    "- ✅ Simple, fast, easy to interpret.\n",
    "- ❌ With many **correlated** columns, weights become unstable (huge positive and negative values that cancel each other) and the model **overfits**: it fits the noise of the training houses.\n",
    "\n",
    "### Regularisation — the cure for overfitting\n",
    "Idea: **punish large weights**. The model now minimises `error + α × penalty(weights)`. The number **α (alpha)** is the strength of the punishment: α = 0 → plain linear regression; large α → very simple model (underfit). We need a good middle value. There are two penalties:\n",
    "\n",
    "### 2) Ridge (L2 penalty = sum of *squared* weights)\n",
    "Shrinks **all** weights smoothly towards 0, but never exactly to 0. When several columns carry the same information, Ridge **shares** the weight between them. \n",
    "- ✅ Stable, great with correlated columns.  ❌ Keeps all ~230 columns, so it is harder to interpret.\n",
    "\n",
    "### 3) Lasso (L1 penalty = sum of *absolute* weights)\n",
    "Pushes the weights of useless columns to **exactly 0**, so it performs **automatic feature selection**: after training, only the useful columns remain.\n",
    "- ✅ Simpler, interpretable model; good when many columns are noise.  ❌ Among a group of correlated columns it tends to keep one and drop the others somewhat arbitrarily.\n",
    "\n",
    "### 4) ElasticNet = Ridge + Lasso mixed\n",
    "`l1_ratio` controls the mix (0 = Ridge, 1 = Lasso). It selects features like Lasso but handles correlated groups more gracefully, like Ridge.\n",
    "\n",
    "> **Why scale first?** The penalty depends on the size of the weights, which depends on the unit of each column (a surface in sq ft vs a 0/1 flag). `RobustScaler` rescales every column using its median and inter-quartile range, so the penalty treats all columns fairly, and extreme values do not disturb it. `make_pipeline(RobustScaler(), model)` simply chains the two steps: scale, then fit — and it re-learns the scaling only on the training part each time.\n",
    ">\n",
    "> **Why `RidgeCV`, `LassoCV`, `ElasticNetCV`?** The \"CV\" versions try many α values by themselves (using cross-validation, explained in Part 5) and keep the best. So we do not have to guess α.\n",
    "\n",
    "---\n",
    "### 5) Random Forest\n",
    "A **decision tree** is a flowchart of yes/no questions (*\"Is `TotalSF` > 2000? → is `OverallQual` > 7? → …\"*) ending with a price. One tree overfits easily. A **forest** builds hundreds of trees, each on a random sample of houses and a random subset of columns, then **averages** their answers. Errors of individual trees cancel out.\n",
    "- ✅ Captures non-linear effects and interactions automatically; little tuning.\n",
    "- ❌ Cannot predict outside the range of prices it has seen; usually less accurate than boosting; slower.\n",
    "\n",
    "### 6) Gradient Boosting\n",
    "Also trees, but built **one after the other**: tree 1 makes a rough prediction; tree 2 is trained to correct the **mistakes** of tree 1; tree 3 corrects what remains, and so on. Each tree is small (`max_depth=3`) and its contribution is shrunk by `learning_rate` (small steps = more careful learning).\n",
    "- ✅ Often the most accurate model on tabular data.\n",
    "- ❌ More settings to tune (`n_estimators`, `learning_rate`, `max_depth`…), can overfit if pushed too far.\n",
    "- `loss=\"huber\"` makes it less sensitive to outliers; `subsample=0.7` trains each tree on 70% of the rows, which adds useful randomness."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
