   "source": [
    "# Part 5 — Cross-validation: a more reliable comparison\n",
    "\n",
    "A single 80/20 split has a weakness: **luck**. The 20% validation houses may happen to be easy or hard. **K-fold cross-validation** removes most of that luck:\n",
    "\n",
    "1. Cut the training data into **5 equal parts** (folds).\n",
    "2. Train on 4 parts, test on the remaining one. Repeat 5 times so that each part is the test part once.\n",
    "3. Average the 5 scores (and look at their spread, `±`).\n",
    "\n",
    "Now every house has been used for testing exactly once, and the average is far more trustworthy. It is also what we use to **choose** between models (we never choose using the Kaggle public leaderboard, which is noisy).\n",
    "\n",
    "`scoring=\"neg_root_mean_squared_error\"`: scikit-learn always maximises a score, so errors are given with a minus sign; we flip the sign back. Since `y` is on the log scale, **this RMSE is the RMSLE**."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "51576470",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "**What this cell does**: runs 5-fold CV for the 6 models on the whole training set and shows the mean RMSLE ± standard deviation. `n_jobs=-1` uses all CPU cores. (It can take a minute or two.)\n",
    "\n",
    "**What to look for**: lowest mean = best. If two models are within one `±` of each other, they are statistically similar."
   ]
  },
