   "source": [
    "## 6.2 Ensemble: averaging different models\n",
    "\n",
    "An **ensemble** combines several models. If two models make **different** mistakes, their average is usually better than either alone — the \"wisdom of the crowd\".\n",
    "\n",
    "We use `VotingRegressor`: it simply computes a **weighted average** of the predictions of its members. We pick models of **two different families**: the three linear ones (Ridge, Lasso, ElasticNet) and the tuned Gradient Boosting (trees). Their errors are not the same, so averaging helps.\n",
    "\n",
    "`weights=[1, 2, 2, 2]` = Ridge counts once, the others twice. (Random Forest and plain Linear Regression are left out: they scored worse, and adding a weak model can hurt.)"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "276af461",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "**What this cell does**: builds the ensemble and measures it with the same 5-fold CV as the others, so the comparison is fair. Then it adds it to the results table.\n",
    "\n",
    "**What to look for**: the ensemble RMSLE should be a bit lower than each member's."
