   "source": [
    "# 📚 Glossary\n",
    "\n",
    "| Term | Plain-English meaning |\n",
    "|---|---|\n",
    "| **Feature** | an input column describing a house (surface, quality…) |\n",
    "| **Target** | the column to predict (`SalePrice`) |\n",
    "| **Regression** | predicting a number |\n",
    "| **Training / validation / test set** | data to learn from / data to check while building / final secret exam |\n",
    "| **Overfitting** | memorising training data instead of learning general rules; great on train, bad on new data |\n",
    "| **Underfitting** | model too simple to capture the pattern |\n",
    "| **Regularisation** | a penalty that keeps model weights small to avoid overfitting (Ridge, Lasso, ElasticNet) |\n",
    "| **Alpha (α)** | the strength of that penalty |\n",
    "| **Cross-validation** | testing the model on several different train/test cuts of the data and averaging |\n",
    "| **Hyperparameter** | a setting chosen by us before training (learning rate, tree depth, α…) |\n",
    "| **GridSearchCV** | automatically tries all combinations of hyperparameters with cross-validation |\n",
    "| **Decision tree** | a flowchart of yes/no questions leading to a prediction |\n",
    "| **Ensemble** | combining several models; errors partly cancel out |\n",
    "| **One-hot encoding** | one 0/1 column per category value |\n",
    "| **Ordinal encoding** | converting ordered labels (Poor < Good < Excellent) to ordered numbers |\n",
    "| **Imputation** | filling missing values |\n",
    "| **Skewness** | asymmetry of a distribution (long tail on one side) |\n",
    "| **log1p / expm1** | `log(1+x)` and its exact inverse; used to tame skewed values |\n",
    "| **Outlier** | an abnormal observation that can mislead the model |\n",
    "| **Data leakage** | information from the validation/test data (especially the answers) accidentally used during training |\n",
    "| **Pipeline** | a chain of steps (e.g. scale → model) applied in order and re-learned on each training part |\n",
    "| **MAE / RMSE / R² / RMSLE** | error metrics, see Part 4.1 |"
   ]
  }
